  "scriptId": "bs-06-swimming-across-flowing-river",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Swimming Across a Flowing River",
    "description": "Solve the chapter’s across-the-river problems by separating across-motion from sideways current.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use a river-width picture instead of upstream/downstream travel. First show the chapter case where the swimmer heads across and is allowed to drift, so across-time is width divided by still-water swimming speed. Then handle landing directly opposite with a right-triangle speed relation and recover current from no-current/current crossing times.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 19, Boats and Streams. Approved scope: Teach the chapter’s two crossing setups. First, when a swimmer heads across the width and is allowed to be carried sideways, crossing time depends on river width and swimming speed in still water; the current changes the landing point, not the across-time. Second, when the swimmer must land directly opposite, part of the swimming speed must cancel the current; use the right-triangle relation behind the chapter formula to find current speed or crossing time. Cover width recovery, sideways drift, the source 100-m river with 4-minute no-current and 5-minute flowing-water times, and feasibility checks that still-water swimming speed must exceed current speed for a directly-opposite landing."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's turn the boat around.\n\nSo far we moved along the river. Now the goal is to cross its width. The current pushes sideways, so we must separate 'across' motion from 'along the river' motion.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A swimmer can swim at 4 km/hr in still water. The river is 1 km wide.\n\nIf he heads straight across the width and allows the current to carry him sideways, his across component remains 4 km/hr.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Crossing time = width ÷ across speed\n= 1/4 hour\n= 15 minutes.\n\nThe current changes where he lands, but in this chapter setup it does not change the time needed to clear the 1 km width.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
